                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1219">s.
                                xiii in</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_957">Conishead,
                                Lancashire, Augustinian priory of St Mary the Virgin</orgName>
                            <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval
                                Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                                Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great
                                Britain index card records the following information, which was
                                deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution: 'A ms. formerly belonging
                                to the monks of the order of St. Augustin at the priory of Conishead
                                in Furness' (s. xviii, inserted paper leaf).</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">H. S. Cowper, <title>Cumberland and
                                Westmorland Antiquarian and Archaeological Society
                                Transactions</title>, N.S. XXVII (1927), pp. 48-53 (with a plate -
                            reduced) contends that it probably remained at Conishead at the
                            Reformation.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Braddyll family s. xviii-xix. <persName
                                role="fmo">John Braddyll</persName> (d. 1728) m. Sarah Dodding,
                            heiress of Conishead.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">At Conishead in 1843. See Jopling,
                                <title>Sketches of Furness and Cartmel</title> (1843), p. 157, with
                            another MS; System of Education for Princes and Nobles grounded upon the
                            Principles of Peripatetic Philosophy.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given by <persName role="fmo">E. R. G.
                                Braddyll</persName> and his sisters to J. S. Estcourt, 1867.
                            [Compare note on reverse of index card: But note that <persName
                                role="fmo">Edward Braddyll</persName> of Whalley owned the Hailes
                            book now belonging to Mr. C. A. Gordon].</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Bought from 'a Leicester bookseller'
                            September 1925 by <persName role="dnr">H. S. Cowper</persName>, who gave
                            it to the museum.</provenance>
                    </history>
